WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.000
Lipid bilayers are nearly impermeable to ions and most polar molecules, and the

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.060
reason is energetic—not a simple matter of size.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.240
Crossing the hydrophobic core means shedding the shell of water an ion or polar

4
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.120
group wears, and stripping that water off costs a lot of energy.

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.400
Hydrophobicity is the key variable.

6
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.040
Indole, the uncharged ring of tryptophan, slips through a membrane far more easily

7
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.800
than tryptophan itself, even though the ring is nearly the same.

8
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.780
Charge is the toll.

9
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.780
Across the whole range, permeability spans about 12 orders of magnitude.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:45.593
Membrane transport proteins sort into three families by speed and energy source.

11
00:00:46.353 --> 00:00:51.353
Channels are the fastest, up to about 10 million ions per second, but passive.

12
00:00:51.873 --> 00:00:55.893
They only let things move down their gradient, with selectivity built into their

13
00:00:55.893 --> 00:00:59.373
structure. Carriers are slower and can be regulated.

14
00:00:59.873 --> 00:01:02.153
They change shape to ferry their cargo across.

15
00:01:02.973 --> 00:01:08.393
Pumps are the slowest, around 100 cycles per second, but they spend ATP to push

16
00:01:08.393 --> 00:01:10.173
molecules against their gradient.

17
00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:15.853
Speed, regulation, and energy source together tell you which machine you are looking

18
00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:16.853
at.

19
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:21.423
The potassium channel moves ions at remarkable speed using a simple trick:

20
00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:23.503
electrostatic repulsion.

21
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:29.443
Several potassium ions sit in the selectivity filter at once, across four binding

22
00:01:29.443 --> 00:01:31.923
sites, S1 through S4.

23
00:01:32.663 --> 00:01:37.743
Because like charges repel, the arrival of one ion shoves the ion ahead of it out

24
00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:43.983
the far side, like balls knocking down an assembly line, or a knock-on turns

25
00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:48.983
binding energy directly into exit energy, so the channel is selective and fast at

26
00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:52.443
the same time, passing potassium at near the diffusion limit.

27
00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:58.277
Secondary active transport comes in two flavors, and both spend the sodium gradient

28
00:01:58.277 --> 00:02:03.997
the pump builds. Symporters move two species in the same direction, SGLT-1 in the

29
00:02:03.997 --> 00:02:07.717
intestine drags glucose uphill by riding sodium downhill.

30
00:02:08.537 --> 00:02:12.937
Anti-porters move them in opposite directions, the cardiac sodium-calcium

31
00:02:12.937 --> 00:02:16.737
exchanger pushes calcium out using the inward sodium flow.

32
00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:22.157
Neither one burns ATP directly; they borrow the energy stored in the sodium gradient

33
00:02:22.157 --> 00:02:24.897
like a waterwheel stealing energy from a current.

34
00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:28.977
Every one of them ultimately depends on the sodium-potassium pump.

35
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.550
Here is a practical decision tree for classifying any transport event.

36
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:38.250
First ask whether the molecule needs to cross at all, and whether it is small and

37
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:43.270
non-polar. If so, it simply diffuses, like oxygen into a red blood cell.

38
00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:47.710
If it needs help, ask which direction it moves relative to its gradient.

39
00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.050
Downhill through a protein is facilitated diffusion or a channel.

40
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:58.910
Uphill means active transport, powered directly by ATP in a pump, or indirectly by

41
00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.690
an ion gradient in secondary active transport.

42
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.890
Three questions sort every case.
